egislature without outside interference\"). It doesn't point to any other rung.\n- **His own words:** his newsletter says he offered \"an amendment to not redraw the maps now and instead establish a non-partisan system of redistricting, in which no political data could be used\". That shows which side he is on. It doesn't say who would draw the maps, so it can't separate rung 1 (citizens' commission, no elected officials) from rung 2 (commission with equal seats for both parties).\n- **Other statements:** the caucus press release and the 2026 forum answers name no rule for who draws the maps, so I coded them `rhetorical`.\n\n`rests_on` is empty because no passage establishes a chair.\n\n**Choices you may want to check:**\n- **Vote page actor quote:** I used the whole Nay-list segment ending in \"Pierce, M\". Another member, \"Pierce, K\", is on the Yea list.\n- **Provision quote:** I used the clause \"The general assembly may amend the congressional districts\". The full sentence on the bill-text page has line numbers inside it, so it isn't verbatim. I didn't quote the deleted commission text.\n- **Forum date:** that source carries two dates. I used 2026-03-23, the date in the file name, because he asks for votes in \"the upcoming May primary\"; the other date is 2026-06-09.\n\n**Missing source:** the text of his December 2025 amendment to HB 1032 and the House vote on it, which I listed in `needs_source`. That amendment might say who draws the maps, which could seat rung 1 or 2.","ttft_ms":25404,"type":"result","duration_ms":53020,"uuid":"b3979f95-b51a-45f7-b2d0-9886babb6c1c","ttft_stream_ms":1631,"time_to_request_ms":73,"first_content_frame_ms":1632,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
